<h2>Seoul's Cafe Culture — A City Obsessed With Coffee</h2>
<p>Seoul has more cafes per capita than almost any city on Earth. This is not an exaggeration. Walk down any street in Hongdae, Seongsu, or Insadong and you'll pass five cafes before you reach the end of the block. Koreans treat cafes as third spaces — places to work, study, meet friends, and spend hours over a single Americano. The coffee is consistently excellent, the interiors are Instagram-worthy, and the prices are reasonable.</p>

<h2>☕ Specialty Coffee Neighborhoods</h2>

<h3>Seongsu-dong — Seoul's Brooklyn</h3>
<p>The trendiest cafe district in Seoul right now. Former industrial warehouses converted into stunning specialty coffee shops. The area attracts Seoul's creative class — designers, artists, and coffee geeks. Key cafes to visit:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Fritz Coffee Company (프릳츠):</strong> Considered one of Korea's best roasters. Their own-roasted beans, excellent pour-overs and espresso. Queue expected on weekends.</li>
  <li><strong>Onyx Coffee Lab:</strong> Seoul outpost of the acclaimed Arkansas roaster. Single origins, precise brewing, serious coffee people.</li>
  <li><strong>Cafe Sukkat:</strong> Gorgeous industrial space. Great for work or Instagram.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Yeonnam-dong</h3>
<p>The quiet, residential neighborhood behind Hongdae has become a specialty coffee paradise. Tree-lined streets, zero tourist crowds, exceptional cafes. Walk the Gyeongui Line Forest Park and stop at cafes along the way.</p>

<h3>Insadong & Bukchon</h3>
<p>Traditional neighborhood cafes with Korean-inspired menus. Try barley latte, roasted grain drinks, and traditional teas alongside espresso. Beautiful hanok (traditional house) cafe settings.</p>

<h2>🎭 Concept & Themed Cafes</h2>
<p>Seoul's concept cafe scene is genuinely unlike anything else in the world:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Blind Whale (Hongdae):</strong> Hidden basement bar-cafe with a speakeasy concept. Ring a doorbell, descend into a dim, book-lined space. Exceptional cocktails and coffee.</li>
  <li><strong>Cafe Bora (Insadong):</strong> Famous for purple charcoal soft-serve ice cream. Queue is long but fast-moving. Perfect Instagram stop.</li>
  <li><strong>Cat Cafes:</strong> Multiple locations in Hongdae and Insadong. Sip coffee while cats sleep on you. ₩10,000–₩15,000 entry includes a drink.</li>
  <li><strong>Sheep Cafe (양카페):</strong> Small sheep wander freely as you drink coffee. Uniquely Korean experience.</li>
  <li><strong>Ground Arcade (Yongsan):</strong> Giant retro gaming cafe — arcade machines, console stations, and good coffee.</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Cafe culture tip:</strong> In Korea, ordering one drink gives you unlimited seating time. Nobody will rush you. Many students spend entire days in cafes studying — "cafe study" (카공족, kagongjok) is a genuine subculture.
</div>

<h2>🧋 Korean Cafe Menu Guide</h2>
<p>Korean cafe menus can be overwhelming. Here's what to order:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Americano (아메리카노):</strong> The most popular drink in Korea. Strong, affordable (₩3,000–₩5,000). Usually iced.</li>
  <li><strong>Dalgona Coffee:</strong> Whipped instant coffee over milk, popularized during COVID. Still found at many cafes.</li>
  <li><strong>Sikhye (식혜):</strong> Sweet fermented rice drink. Unique Korean flavor — try it once.</li>
  <li><strong>Bingsu (빙수):</strong> Finely shaved ice with toppings. Mango, strawberry, matcha, or red bean. Huge portions, usually shared.</li>
  <li><strong>Egg Tart:</strong> Portuguese-style custard tart, adopted and perfected by Korean bakeries. Get one from Paris Baguette or Tous Les Jours.</li>
</ul>

<h2>💻 Best Cafes for Working</h2>
<p>Seoul cafes are incredibly laptop-friendly. What to look for:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Free WiFi is universal and fast (look for the password on your receipt or ask)</li>
  <li>Power outlets at most tables in Seongsu and Hongdae cafes</li>
  <li><strong>Best for work:</strong> Anthracite (multiple locations), Cafe Mamas (Mapo-gu), any large Fritz location</li>
  <li>Avoid tiny dessert cafes for work sessions — go for larger spaces</li>
</ul>

<div class="tip-box">
  💡 <strong>Budget tip:</strong> Convenience store coffee from GS25 or 7-Eleven is surprisingly decent — fresh-ground coffee machines for ₩1,000–₩1,500. For a budget cafe, look for Mega Coffee or Compose Coffee chains — large drinks for ₩2,000–₩3,000.
</div>

<h2>📸 Most Photogenic Cafes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Zapangi (자판기 카페):</strong> Hidden vending machine entrance in Yongsan. Inside: vast, architecturally stunning space.</li>
  <li><strong>Skygarden Terrace Cafes:</strong> Rooftop cafes overlooking the Seoullo 7017 elevated park.</li>
  <li><strong>Cafe Yeonnam-dong 239-20:</strong> Trompe-l'oeil illustrated interior — everything looks hand-drawn. Hugely popular for photos.</li>
  <li><strong>Osulloc Tea House (Insadong):</strong> Green tea everything. Stunning minimalist design. Jeju Island brand.</li>
</ul>

<h2>💴 What to Budget</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Budget cafe chains (Mega, Compose): ₩1,500–₩3,000</li>
  <li>Standard café Americano: ₩3,500–₩5,000</li>
  <li>Specialty pour-over: ₩7,000–₩12,000</li>
  <li>Concept cafe entry + drink: ₩10,000–₩20,000</li>
  <li>Bingsu (shared): ₩15,000–₩25,000</li>
</ul>

